# A demonstration phrase, written for this tool.
#
# Nothing here is sampled, transcribed or borrowed: it is eight bars of my own
# arithmetic, chosen to exercise the four channels and the hardware behaviours a
# naive tone generator gets wrong. Render it with:
#
#     terminalgb-chiptune docs/chiptune-examples/demo.gbscore -o demo.wav
#
# and see what the chip is really told with:
#
#     terminalgb-chiptune docs/chiptune-examples/demo.gbscore --dump-registers

title Four channels, one Game Boy
tempo 132
model dmg

# --- Channel 1: a C major scale, then an arpeggio, then a sweep ------------
# A plucked lead: full volume, decaying with envelope period 3 (~187 ms to
# silence), 50% duty.
ch1 duty 2
ch1 env 15 - 3

ch1 note C4 1/8
ch1 note D4 1/8
ch1 note E4 1/8
ch1 note F4 1/8
ch1 note G4 1/8
ch1 note A4 1/8
ch1 note B4 1/8
ch1 note C5 1/8

ch1 duty 1
ch1 note C4 1/8
ch1 note E4 1/8
ch1 note G4 1/8
ch1 note C5 1/8
ch1 note G4 1/8
ch1 note E4 1/8
ch1 note C4 1/4

# The frequency sweep — channel 1's, and nothing else's. Period 3, downward,
# shift 2: the classic falling "landing" sound. A tone generator with an
# envelope but no sweep unit cannot produce this at all.
ch1 sweep 3 - 2
ch1 env 15 - 2
ch1 note C5 1/2
ch1 sweep off

# ...and upward, which is the same unit run the other way. A gentle shift, on
# purpose: the sweep adds `period >> shift` to the period register each step, so
# a big shift climbs fast and overflows past 2047 — at which point the hardware
# **switches the channel off**. `sweep 4 + 3` from here is silent after 30 ms,
# and that is correct, not a bug. It is also how the classic vanishing
# "power-up" is made.
ch1 sweep 5 + 6
ch1 note C4 1/2
ch1 sweep off

# --- Channel 2: a bass line under all of it -------------------------------
ch2 duty 1
ch2 env 12 - 2
ch2 note C3 1/4
ch2 note C3 1/4
ch2 note G2 1/4
ch2 note G2 1/4
ch2 note C3 1/4
ch2 note C3 1/4
ch2 note F2 1/4
ch2 note G2 1/4
ch2 note C3 1/2
ch2 note G2 1/2

# --- Channel 3: a wavetable pad -------------------------------------------
# 32 four-bit samples: a triangle. Channel 3 plays whatever is in wave RAM, so
# this is the one channel whose *timbre* is data rather than a duty setting.
ch3 wave 0123456789ABCDEFFEDCBA9876543210
ch3 level 2
ch3 note C3 1/1
ch3 note F2 1/1

# A sawtooth on the same channel, to hear the difference the table makes.
ch3 wave 00112233445566778899AABBCCDDEEFF
ch3 note C3 1/1
ch3 note G2 1/1

# --- Channel 4: the drum kit ----------------------------------------------
# Noise is an LFSR, not a random-number generator: the 7-bit width gives the
# short, pitched, metallic rattle a snare is made of, and the 15-bit width the
# long hiss of a hi-hat. Same channel, one bit of difference.
ch4 env 15 - 2
ch4 noise 4 15 4
ch4 hit 1/8
ch4 rest 1/8
ch4 noise 2 7 3
ch4 hit 1/8
ch4 rest 1/8

ch4 noise 4 15 4
ch4 hit 1/8
ch4 rest 1/8
ch4 noise 2 7 3
ch4 hit 1/8
ch4 rest 1/8

ch4 env 15 - 1
ch4 noise 6 15 6
ch4 hit 1/4
ch4 rest 1/4
ch4 noise 2 7 3
ch4 hit 1/8
ch4 hit 1/8
ch4 hit 1/8
ch4 hit 1/8

tail 400ms
